# The parts a filing draws from its own stack. One row per part whose notation is its own
# composition's, so it names a layer that same document built.
#
# XSD 1.0 cannot make a keyref conditional
#   XSD 1.0 cannot make a keyref conditional on another field's value, which is why this set exists
#   as a query rather than as a constraint. `composedLayerName` cannot be pointed at "the local
#   stack, but only when the notation matches mine", so a local part naming a layer that is not
#   there validates cleanly, and so does a local part that closes a loop.
#
# It stops one step short of composition/parts.sqlc, on purpose
#   `composition/parts.sqlc` resolves a part: it keeps the ones whose notation names a filing here
#   AND whose layer is in that filing, and projects the resolved filing. This relation does the
#   first half and not the second, so a part naming a layer its own document never built is still
#   a row. It has to be: `checks/local_part_dangles.sqlc` is the rule about exactly those, and a
#   relation that had already dropped them would leave that rule nothing to examine.
#
# The loop is the one that would hang a reader
#   Layer A composed from B while B is composed from A is a document with no arithmetic in it:
#   every figure depends on itself. A foreign part cannot do that within one document; a local one
#   can, and that is the cost of the capability. Both local-part rules examine exactly this
#   relation.
-- pm.part where pm:ForeignId/notation equals the composition's own pm:notation.
SELECT p.*
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/part_references.sqlc)
) p
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/notations.sqlc)
) fi ON fi.filing = p.composition AND fi.notation = p.part_filing
